 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue lifecycle
+
+Queues are created and maintained by managers only (owner/admin of the entity):
+
+- **Create** (`App\Actions\CreateQueue`): name is trimmed, 1–255 chars; slug defaults to `Str::slug(name)` (`Atendimento Geral` → `atendimento-geral`) and an explicit slug is slugified the same way. New queues are active (`is_active = true`).
+- **Slug uniqueness** is per entity: the same slug may exist in another entity, but a duplicate within the entity is a `ValidationException` — including a DB unique-index hit from a race, which is converted instead of leaking a `QueryException`.
+- **Update** (`App\Actions\UpdateQueue`): any combination of new name, new slug, and active flag (named, nullable parameters; `null` means "leave unchanged"). Keeping the queue's own slug is fine; taking another queue's slug is a `ValidationException`.
+- **No hard delete.** Deactivation (`is_active = false`) is how a queue is retired: its tickets are preserved, and issuing or calling on it keeps failing with `InactiveQueueException`. There is no queue delete/remove/destroy action.
+- Employees, members of other entities, and non-members get `AuthorizationException`; nothing is written.
+
+### Per-queue access grants
+
+Employees never get blanket access — a manager decides which employees may call tickets on which queue (`App\Actions\SetQueueAccess`, table `queue_access`):
+
+- The only permission today is `call` (`App\Enums\QueuePermission`).
+- Setting a non-empty permission list upserts the `(entity_membership_id, queue_id)` row; setting an empty list revokes (deletes) it. Unknown strings or non-string entries are a `ValidationException` and nothing is stored. Membership and queue of different entities are a `ValidationException`. Non-managers (including an employee granting themselves) get `AuthorizationException`.
+- Every grant/revoke is audited on the `audit` log channel (`queue.access.granted` / `queue.access.revoked` with `actor_id`, `queue_id`, `entity_membership_id`). A revoke with no row to delete is a no-op and is not logged.
+
+### Ticket-call authorization (`App\Policies\QueuePolicy`, single source of truth)
+
+| Ability | Who may |
+| ------- | ------- |
+| `create` (entity as argument) | Owner/admin of that entity |
+| `update`, `manageAccess` | Owner/admin of the queue's entity |
+| `call` | Owner/admin of the queue's entity always (no grant row needed); an employee only with a `queue_access` row for **that** queue whose `permissions` array contains `call` (a corrupted non-array value denies); everyone else denied |
+
+Both call paths enforce `call` after locking the queue and confirming it is active: `CallNextTicket` authorizes the caller in every call mode (unauthorized → `AuthorizationException`, no ticket changes), and `Ticket::call()` rejects inactive queues with `InactiveQueueException`, then authorizes (`AuthorizationException`, even when the ticket was already called), then applies the existing "only waiting tickets" `LogicException`.
+
 ### Ticket
 
 Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (filled when called). Attributes:
